Tennis elbow is one of the most common problems we see in clinic — and despite the name, most people who have it don't play tennis. It's just as likely to follow a weekend of renovating, a busy month on the tools, a new gym program or long days on a keyboard and mouse.

## What is tennis elbow?

Tennis elbow — known clinically as **lateral elbow tendinopathy** or lateral epicondylalgia — is pain on the outside of the elbow where the forearm muscles that extend the wrist and fingers attach to the bone. It's usually an overload problem: the tendon has been asked to do more than it was ready for, and it becomes painful and less tolerant of load.

**Common signs include:**

- Pain on the outer elbow, sometimes spreading down the forearm
- Pain gripping, lifting a kettle or carrying shopping with the palm down
- Pain shaking hands, turning a doorknob or using a screwdriver
- Weaker grip than usual

It can settle on its own, but for many people it lingers for months. The good news is that there are well-researched ways to speed things up.

## What the research says about dry needling

Dry needling uses very fine, sterile needles placed into tight, tender areas of the forearm muscles. Two systematic reviews pooling the clinical trials have looked specifically at tennis elbow:

- A 2020 meta-analysis of seven trials (320 patients) found that dry needling **reduced pain intensity**, with the most significant effect in the short term.
- An updated 2024 meta-analysis found improvements in **pain within the first week, elbow function and grip strength** — and that a local "twitch" response during needling appeared important when targeting trigger points.

In practice, we use dry needling to settle pain and muscle sensitivity so that you can start loading the tendon with exercise sooner — which is what builds lasting tolerance.

## Why we may also treat your neck, upper back, ribs and shoulder

The nerves supplying the elbow and forearm come from the neck, and the upper back, ribs and shoulder all influence how the arm moves and takes load. That's why we examine the whole upper quarter, not just the sore spot.

The research supports this approach:

- An early trial found that a single manual treatment to the **neck** produced immediate improvements in pain and grip strength in people with tennis elbow.
- A systematic review found **moderate evidence** for short- and mid-term benefit from manipulation of the **cervical and thoracic spine** when added to stretching, strengthening and mobilisation of the wrist and forearm.
- Reviews of manual therapy also support **mobilisation with movement** at the elbow, with benefits shown in the short term and beyond.

Depending on your assessment, care may include Diversified manipulation or mobilisation of the neck, upper back and ribs, and treatment around the shoulder and elbow — always explained first, and only with your consent.

## Exercise is the backbone of recovery

Hands-on care helps calm things down; **progressive loading** is what helps the tendon adapt. A typical program includes:

- Isometric holds (gripping or wrist-extension holds without movement) to ease pain
- Slow wrist-extension strengthening, gradually increased
- Grip and forearm rotation exercises
- Strengthening for the shoulder and upper back

## Everyday tips while it settles

- **Modify, don't stop.** Reduce the aggravating task where you can, rather than resting the arm completely.
- **Grip with the palm up** when lifting where possible, and use two hands.
- **Check your tools and set-up** — tool handle size, mouse position and keyboard height all matter.
- **Be patient with injections.** Research comparing approaches found that corticosteroid injections gave quick short-term relief but poorer outcomes at one year than physiotherapy-style care or a wait-and-see approach — something worth discussing with your GP.

## When to see a doctor first

Book in with your GP if elbow pain follows a fall or significant injury, if the elbow is hot, red or swollen, if you have numbness or pins and needles in the hand, or if you feel generally unwell.

## How we can help

At Malak Chiropractic, we'll confirm whether your pain fits tennis elbow (and rule out pain referred from the neck), explain what's driving it, and agree a plan that may combine dry needling, manual therapy to the neck, upper back, ribs and shoulder, and a progressive exercise program. Every person responds differently, so we review progress regularly and adjust as we go.
